  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAfternoon water vapor imagery and RAP analysis feature troughing \nover the Pacific NW, a couple of shortwaves over the Plains, and \nridging over the Canadian Prairie. At the surface, this is \nsupporting high pressure currently centered over Manitoba, which is \nin turn inducing northerly flow over Superior. While most of the UP \nremains dry, the lake effect rain showers, with the help of some \nupsloping off of Superior, have been able to develop mainly over the \nnorth-central UP. Mainly trace accumulations are expected in any \nrain showers this afternoon. Onshore flow and lake clouds have kept \ntemperatures cooler than expected in the northern UP, where we \nshould only see highs in the mid/upper 40s. Elsewhere, temperatures \nare rising into the upper 40s, and some spots may peak near 50F. \n\nTonight into Tuesday, the shortwave currently over NE/SD moves \nnortheastward, with a surface trough our even a weak surface low \nclosing off and moving into MN by Tuesday afternoon. WAA out ahead \nof this brings our next opportunity for rain showers late tonight, \nlingering through Tuesday as the wave makes its way into Ontario. \nRain chances are greatest over the western UP, closer in proximity \nto our better forcing, but some shower activity is still possible \neast of M-95. Still, rain showers remain light, with totals through \nTuesday generally of a tenth of an inch or less. Expect otherwise \nbreezy conditions, as winds out of the SE gust up to 20mph across \nmost of the UP. Temperatures bottom out in the lower to mid 30s for \nmost, though some of the typically cooler spots in the north-central \nUP may fall back below freezing tonight. Expect highs to come in a \nfew degrees warmer than today, reaching the lower to mid 50s for \nmost. \n\nAnother more vigorous shortwave lifts into Ontario Wednesday, \nfurther pushing temps back into the 60s to near 70. This drags a \ncold front through the UP Wednesday night through Thursday morning, \nkicking off better chances for showers. Deterministic models and \ntheir ensemble counterparts generally agree on quiet weather through \nmuch of the weekend as ridging recovers over the region, potentially \nkeeping Halloween night dry. Guidance begins to diverge the first \ndays of November as a more zonal/SW flow aloft pattern takes shape, \nsending shortwave activity back into the region. At this time, \nensembles don't suggest any widespread meaningful rain across the \narea with 24hr prob of QPF >0.1\" only reaching 60-80% on any given \nday for portions of the UP. Additionally, above normal temperatures \ndominate through the rest of the month into early November with \nseemingly no end in sight as the CPC extended probabilities favor \nabove normal temps through the 1-3 week and 3-4 week outlooks. All \nglobal ensembles highlight strong positive 850mb temp anomalies even \ninto their extended 200+ hour range.",
